  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Cold temperatures aloft and minimal \nbuoyancy should support isolated thunderstorm formation in most of\nNorthern and Central Nevada this afternoon. The primary\nthunderstorm hazards will be locally heavy rainfall, wind gusts \nnear 45 mph, and dangerous cloud to ground lightning strikes. An\nupper-level area of low pressure will approach Northern and\nCentral Nevada tonight. This cyclone aloft will move eastward\nacross the forecast area Sunday. As a result, periods of valley\nrain and mountain snow are anticipated this afternoon through\nSunday evening. The majority of precipitation will fall tonight\nand Sunday morning. Snow levels will start at around 8,000 feet \nthis afternoon, before dropping to about 6,000 feet Sunday \nmorning, resulting in rain changing to snow on most passes and\nsummits along Highway 50. Here are probabilities of one half of an\ninch or more of rain for select locations in Northern and Central\nNevada from 11 AM PDT Saturday, October 31st, 2026 through 10 PM\nPDT Sunday, November 1st, 2026: \n\nEly - 99%\nLamoille - 86%\nOwyhee - 82%\nSpring Creek - 73%\nBattle Mountain - 72% \nWinnemucca - 65% \nElko - 65% \nWells - 61%\nCarlin - 60%\nJackpot - 55%\n\nPrecipitation will come to an end early Monday morning at the\nlatest. A cooling trend is expected this weekend. By Sunday\nafternoon, high temperatures throughout Northern and Central\nNevada will be eleven to thirteen degrees below normal for this\ntime of year. After a brief period of dry weather, another storm\nsystem may spread precipitation eastward across Northern and\nCentral Nevada Tuesday night into Wednesday. Yet another low\npressure system may impact Northern Nevada late next week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High forecast confidence\nregarding periods of valley rain and mountain snow this afternoon\nthrough Sunday evening. Moderate forecast confidence in the \npossibility of a few strong thunderstorms this afternoon. Moderate\nforecast confidence regarding a marginal risk of excessive \nrainfall this afternoon through Sunday morning. High forecast \nconfidence in a cooling trend this weekend. No deviations from the\nNBM model output.",
